A file comment header is the introductory comment at the top of a source file. It can identify the file, project, author, date, or license. It is separate from a C++ `.hpp` file or an include guard.

To create a complete `LICENSE` file, use the separate [project license step](/docs/licenses/) in the creation wizard. It can supply the initial SPDX and copyright fields below. Editing a header alone does not license or relicense the project.

## Personalize during creation

In `:RivetNew`, choose **Personalize and preview styles** when asked about file comment headers. Rivet asks for your details before showing the style previews:

| Field | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| Author, email, organization | Optional credits; blank fields are omitted. |
| Creation date | `today` uses the date each new file is generated. A fixed `YYYY-MM-DD` uses that date; blank omits it. |
| Copyright notice | Your supplied text, such as `2026 Ada Lovelace`. Rivet does not infer an owner from the author or organization. |
| SPDX license expression | Your supplied identifier or expression. Blank omits licensing text. |
| Description | An optional summary shared by new files. It can be customized per file later. |

These details are saved in the project's `.rivet.json` and inserted into generated source files accepted by the language's header service: C/C++, Python, Rust, Swift, or Lua. Enter only details you want in those project files. Rivet does not look up your identity or choose a license for you.

## Compare the styles

The picker renders each style with your actual details and the preview filename. Move with `j` and `k`, scroll the preview with Tab, and select with Enter. The final project review includes the actual header in every generated file; `c` or Ctrl-S approves creation.

These examples use **Ada Lovelace** as a placeholder. Rivet leaves the real author field blank until you supply it.

**Compact** keeps the metadata in a short sequence of line comments:

```cpp
// main.cpp — engine
// Author: Ada Lovelace
// Created: 2026-10-09
```

**Xcode-style** separates the filename, project, and credits with blank comment lines:

```cpp
//
//  main.cpp
//  engine
//
//  Author: Ada Lovelace
//  Created: 2026-10-09
//
```

**Doxygen documentation** is the recommended C/C++ API-documentation choice. It uses C-style file documentation tags. Python offers line-comment styles instead:

```cpp
/**
 * @file main.cpp
 * @par Project: engine
 * @author Ada Lovelace
 * @date 2026-10-09
 * @brief Application entry point.
 */
```

**SPDX first** puts the supplied license expression and optional copyright notice at the top. This choice appears only after you enter a license expression. For example, if you supply `MIT` and `2026 Ada Lovelace`:

```cpp
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Ada Lovelace
// File: main.cpp (engine)
// Author: Ada Lovelace
```

Python's compact, Xcode-style, and SPDX headers use `#` lines; Lua uses `--` lines and `---` documentation lines; C/C++, Rust, and Swift use `//` or block comments. Selecting **No header** omits these file-header comments. C++ formatting can adjust them to the selected `clang-format` style; the final review shows the resulting text.

## Add or edit one file

Open a supported source file inside a generated project and run `:RivetHeader`. Project defaults prefill the inputs. Recognized fields in an existing header take precedence, including its original creation date. Python shebangs and encoding declarations, and Swift's leading tools-version directive, retain their required positions.

If the file begins with a standalone comment after any required preamble, choose whether to replace that comment or keep it and prepend a header. Rivet shows a diff and the complete resulting file before approval. It considers only the initial C-style comment block or consecutive line comments (`//`, Python's `#`, or Lua's `--`); it does not search through the file for comments to remove. Lua long comments are preserved.

Press `c` or Ctrl-S to update the buffer. Save with `:write`, or undo with `u`. Canceling a field or review leaves the buffer alone. If the buffer changes while the review is open, Rivet refuses the stale edit and asks you to start again.

To remove a header, choose to replace the leading comment, then select **No header** and review the removal. Comments sharing a line with code are kept; Rivet will offer to add a separate header above them.

## Change future defaults

Run `:RivetHeaderDefaults` to change the project's saved details and style through the same inputs and previews. The final review updates `.rivet.json`. Existing source files keep their current headers; new files and targets use the new defaults.

The Neovim commands provide the interactive header editor. The CLI's file and target generators also honor saved project header defaults, but there are no CLI header-editing flags.

## Preview headers when adding files

`:RivetFile` shows the configured header in the template picker for every supported source language. Python, Rust, and Swift use a **New source file** picker with one **Source file** choice before the path prompt; C/C++ and Lua show the header beside their existing template choices. That filename is an example; the final review renders every generated file with its actual filename, including both halves of a source/header or module interface/implementation pair.

Files in another language use that language's comment syntax for the project's saved header details, such as Python's `#` or Lua's `--`. If no header is configured, including an ordinary folder without saved defaults, the preview says so. Set future-file defaults with `:RivetHeaderDefaults` in a Rivet-owned project; edit a single existing file with `:RivetHeader`.

A Doxygen file header is the starting point. Add documentation for public functions and types, then generate HTML/XML as shown in [Document your API with Doxygen](/docs/doxygen/). Python, Rust, Swift, and Lua use their own comment conventions; a Doxygen integration for unsupported languages requires an explicit filter or adapter.
